WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.273
So, hi, and welcome to our webcast,

00:00:04.273 --> 00:00:08.554
MBA Student Insights LGBT at the GSB.

00:00:08.554 --> 00:00:14.207
My name's Marco, and I'm a second
year MBA student here at the GSB,

00:00:14.207 --> 00:00:20.250
and we'll be talking about the LGBT
student experience here on campus and

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:23.681
try to answer as many questions as we can.

00:00:23.681 --> 00:00:27.480
To submit a question to us about
what it's like to be a student here,

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:32.690
just click the Chat button at the top
of your screen, enter your question,

00:00:32.690 --> 00:00:35.360
and click Send to All Panelists.

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:39.330
So click the Chat button at the top,
enter your question, and

00:00:39.330 --> 00:00:41.160
enter Send to All Panelists.

00:00:42.198 --> 00:00:47.760
We're not going to be talking about
the admissions process, the application,

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:51.500
or financial aid, but there are staff
from the MBA Admissions Office that

00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:55.215
are going to answer those types of
questions directly in the chat box itself.

00:00:56.637 --> 00:01:00.015
All right, so first things first,
we're going to start off with some intros.

00:01:00.015 --> 00:01:03.605
I have Wynn, and Marcella here.

00:01:03.605 --> 00:01:07.895
Why don't you start by telling us
a bit about where you're from,

00:01:07.895 --> 00:01:12.770
what you did before coming to the GSB,
and what your goals are after graduation.

00:01:12.770 --> 00:01:15.300
&gt;&gt; Awesome, I guess I'll kick us off.

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:17.350
My name is Wynn Chessen.

00:01:17.350 --> 00:01:19.260
I'm so excited to be on this panel.

00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:21.460
I grew up in Chapel Hill, North Carolina.

00:01:21.460 --> 00:01:22.710
That's where I came out.

00:01:22.710 --> 00:01:27.220
I went to the University of North Carolina
at Chapel Hill for undergrad.

00:01:27.220 --> 00:01:29.726
Studied womens studies,
gender studies, and sexuality studies.

00:01:29.726 --> 00:01:32.038
And then I went to work
in New York City for

00:01:32.038 --> 00:01:35.446
an LGBT rights non-profit
called Immigration Equality.

00:01:35.446 --> 00:01:38.928
So, all my professional
experience prior to

00:01:38.928 --> 00:01:43.602
the GSB was actually focused
on championing LGBT equality,

00:01:43.602 --> 00:01:48.380
especially for immigrant
communities in the United States.

00:01:48.380 --> 00:01:52.364
And I'm a career-switcher so
I'm not exactly sure what's next for me,

00:01:52.364 --> 00:01:56.938
but I'm really excited to be at the GSB,
to have so many amazing new opportunities.

00:01:56.938 --> 00:02:00.372
And so, ask me in a year where I am.

00:02:00.372 --> 00:02:02.038
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:02:02.038 --> 00:02:02.668
&gt;&gt; Marcella.

00:02:02.668 --> 00:02:03.318
&gt;&gt; Marcella.

00:02:03.318 --> 00:02:07.030
&gt;&gt; Hello everyone,
also very excited to be here.

00:02:07.030 --> 00:02:08.120
My name is Marcella.

00:02:08.120 --> 00:02:10.738
I am from Mexico.
I went to Monterey Tech where I studied

00:02:10.738 --> 00:02:12.357
industrial engineering,

00:02:12.357 --> 00:02:17.369
then had a more traditional background
doing management consulting for two years.

00:02:17.369 --> 00:02:22.357
But I'm now, after the GSB,
going to go work at The Nature Conservancy

00:02:22.357 --> 00:02:28.040
which is an environmental nonprofit,
and I'm very excited about that.

00:02:28.040 --> 00:02:29.178
Also glad to be here today.

00:02:29.178 --> 00:02:31.540
&gt;&gt; Perfect, thanks Marcella.

00:02:31.540 --> 00:02:34.550
So as for myself, like I said,
my name's Marco Chan.

00:02:34.550 --> 00:02:38.740
I'm a second year here at the GSB, but
coincidentally just like Wynn I'm a dual

00:02:38.740 --> 00:02:42.900
degree student in public administration
at the Kennedy School as well.

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:46.380
In terms of before school,
I studied romance studies.

00:02:46.380 --> 00:02:49.515
Or actually, I'm from Canada,
so you have NAFTA up here.

00:02:49.515 --> 00:02:52.540
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And I also lived for a while in Macau.

00:02:52.540 --> 00:02:56.040
In terms of educational background,
I studied romance studies at Harvard.

00:02:56.040 --> 00:02:59.618
And then worked at Bain &amp; Company in
consulting for a couple of years.

00:02:59.618 --> 00:03:03.008
And was at the World Bank for
a bit in International development.

00:03:03.008 --> 00:03:07.904
And then long-term, I'm considering
either LGBT advocacy full-time,

00:03:07.904 --> 00:03:12.118
or I'm also really interested
in Canadian politics and policy.

00:03:12.118 --> 00:03:13.458
So,
&gt;&gt; Awesome.

00:03:13.458 --> 00:03:14.474
&gt;&gt; Very dissimilar things.

00:03:14.474 --> 00:03:16.668
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:03:16.668 --> 00:03:18.604
&gt;&gt; All right, well, so with that,

00:03:18.604 --> 00:03:24.230
why don't we just get started with some
questions that the audience has submitted.

00:03:24.230 --> 00:03:27.512
So, why don't we start with the first one,

00:03:27.512 --> 00:03:30.721
what is the LGBT community
like at the GSB?

00:03:30.721 --> 00:03:31.473
&gt;&gt; Do you want me to start?

00:03:31.473 --> 00:03:33.383
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:03:33.383 --> 00:03:38.980
&gt;&gt; So,
I should start by saying I love the GSB.

00:03:38.980 --> 00:03:41.900
And, one of the most
important communities for

00:03:41.900 --> 00:03:44.747
me since I've gotten
here has been GSB pride,

00:03:44.747 --> 00:03:49.128
and that's kind of the all-inclusive
MBA one, MBA two and MBA three.

00:03:49.128 --> 00:03:49.833
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; MSX.

00:03:49.833 --> 00:03:50.680
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:03:50.680 --> 00:03:52.756
&gt;&gt; PhD.
&gt;&gt; One year students, PhD students.

00:03:52.756 --> 00:03:56.701
And, I think it's because
they host a retreat

00:03:56.701 --> 00:03:59.845
very soon after you get onto campus.

00:03:59.845 --> 00:04:04.750
So as you're kind of establishing yourself
and trying to make friends with section

00:04:04.750 --> 00:04:09.370
mates, it's kind of nice to break away
from that from a minute and get to meet,

00:04:09.370 --> 00:04:13.782
not just people of your year outside
of your section, but also get to meet,

00:04:13.782 --> 00:04:16.334
start friendships that are MBA twos, or.

00:04:16.334 --> 00:04:20.417
I met both of you at that retreat,
and that was just really meaningful.

00:04:20.417 --> 00:04:22.002
So that's kind of how it started for me.

00:04:22.002 --> 00:04:23.180
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; Cool.

00:04:23.180 --> 00:04:29.203
&gt;&gt; I really wanted to let Wynn go first,
because as he mentioned, I'm an MBA three.

00:04:29.203 --> 00:04:32.120
The program is usually two
years as I'm sure you know, but

00:04:32.120 --> 00:04:35.937
I joined the Greek candidate with
the School of Earth, Energy, and

00:04:35.937 --> 00:04:38.840
Environmental Studies, so E-IPER.

00:04:38.840 --> 00:04:41.104
So I wanted to provide
the historic perspective.

00:04:41.104 --> 00:04:43.820
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] And it seemed really exciting

00:04:43.820 --> 00:04:49.550
to see how the GSB pride
community has changed.

00:04:49.550 --> 00:04:52.596
There were fewer applause when I started,
and it was a more,

00:04:52.596 --> 00:04:54.587
in a way a more homogeneous community.

00:04:54.587 --> 00:04:56.816
And I'm really excited to see, and

00:04:56.816 --> 00:05:00.618
I hope you'll talk about prom
where you got to wear a dragon.

00:05:00.618 --> 00:05:01.732
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I just,

00:05:01.732 --> 00:05:06.586
[LAUGH] Feel like the conversations and
the experience here being so

00:05:06.586 --> 00:05:09.195
much more inclusive and stronger.

00:05:09.195 --> 00:05:14.434
And again, coming back to the fact that
I'm an MBA three, it's really exciting

00:05:14.434 --> 00:05:19.227
to make friends with the first years
even now after my friends graduated.

00:05:19.227 --> 00:05:20.259
And then with the second years.

00:05:20.259 --> 00:05:23.852
And GSB Pride has really
been at the core of that.

00:05:23.852 --> 00:05:27.710
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think as for
my thoughts on what the LGBT

00:05:27.710 --> 00:05:32.900
community's been like here,
I'll offer two things.

00:05:32.900 --> 00:05:35.997
I think the first is that for
me, partly because my social and

00:05:35.997 --> 00:05:40.218
professional interest is in the LGBT
community, it always feels like home base.

00:05:40.218 --> 00:05:43.783
So it's nice that we have a group
where it's big enough that,

00:05:43.783 --> 00:05:47.693
to your points there's quite a bit
of diversity in our group in terms

00:05:47.693 --> 00:05:51.347
of work background,
where people grew up, ideologies even.

00:05:51.347 --> 00:05:55.408
There's enough of that, but it's also a
tight-knit enough group where I feel like

00:05:55.408 --> 00:05:58.396
I really do know every single
person to one degree or another.

00:05:58.396 --> 00:06:02.004
So it's nice to have,
we get together every Thursday for

00:06:02.004 --> 00:06:06.964
drinks in different people's houses,
we have events all the time, we'll go

00:06:06.964 --> 00:06:11.567
together to different Pan Stanford or
local San Francisco LGBT events.

00:06:11.567 --> 00:06:13.782
So, it's nice to have that community.

00:06:13.782 --> 00:06:16.894
And then I think the second
thing I'll share is that,

00:06:16.894 --> 00:06:19.799
again, to what Marcella said,
I think frankly,

00:06:19.799 --> 00:06:24.590
everyone's kind of surprised by how
international our LGBT experience is.

00:06:24.590 --> 00:06:29.520
We have people from China,
India, Saudi Arabia, Canada,

00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:30.170
Mexico,
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:06:30.170 --> 00:06:31.560
&gt;&gt; Brazil.

00:06:31.560 --> 00:06:34.680
So I think it's really enriched
discussions that we've had,

00:06:34.680 --> 00:06:40.260
particularly with allies, around what it
means for us to think about inclusion and

00:06:40.260 --> 00:06:43.880
diversity in our own personal lives,
but also in the workplace.

00:06:43.880 --> 00:06:47.912
So all these different places that people
from the GSB will ultimately end up

00:06:47.912 --> 00:06:48.810
back at.

00:06:48.810 --> 00:06:49.780
So, that's a piece I'll share around that.

00:06:49.780 --> 00:06:52.694
The second question that we have is,

00:06:52.694 --> 00:06:57.212
how did you know it was the right time for
you to get an MBA?

00:06:57.212 --> 00:06:58.775
Why wasn't that five years ago?

00:06:58.775 --> 00:07:00.316
Why wasn't that two years later?

00:07:03.475 --> 00:07:06.722
&gt;&gt; I thought again,
being in management consulting,

00:07:06.722 --> 00:07:11.238
I thought that I was under a deadline
to apply before my two years were up.

00:07:11.238 --> 00:07:15.222
It turned out maybe that wasn't
quite what I thought it was.

00:07:15.222 --> 00:07:18.955
But I just thought I had
to do it at that point.

00:07:18.955 --> 00:07:21.312
The right time is probably when
you feel it's the right time.

00:07:21.312 --> 00:07:23.226
I'm super glad I got to do it then,

00:07:23.226 --> 00:07:27.847
because the GSB has really allowed me to
leverage what I had learned in consulting,

00:07:27.847 --> 00:07:31.493
but really transition to a career
that's very meaningful to me.

00:07:31.493 --> 00:07:32.399
So, in a way,

00:07:32.399 --> 00:07:35.151
I want to say the sooner the better,
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:07:35.151 --> 00:07:38.475
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] But whenever it's right for

00:07:38.475 --> 00:07:39.052
you.

00:07:39.052 --> 00:07:44.481
&gt;&gt; For me it was, if you kind of rewinded
back seven years and asked me would I

00:07:44.481 --> 00:07:49.567
be getting an MBA, I think my
answer would've been absolutely no.

00:07:49.567 --> 00:07:52.673
But the more I worked in
the LGBT rights world,

00:07:52.673 --> 00:07:56.895
the more value I saw as kind of
the organization I was working for

00:07:56.895 --> 00:08:00.737
partnered more and
more with Fortune 500 companies.

00:08:00.737 --> 00:08:03.923
And When I kind of decided
to come back to school,

00:08:03.923 --> 00:08:07.358
it was after an enormous victory for
our community.

00:08:07.358 --> 00:08:12.790
And I think the United States Supreme
Court issued the Windsor decision,

00:08:12.790 --> 00:08:17.697
and one of the three things
Immigration Equality was working on was

00:08:17.697 --> 00:08:22.795
being able to apply for a green card for
your same sex husband or wife.

00:08:22.795 --> 00:08:26.578
And so, once we won that key victory,
obviously there's a lot more to do.

00:08:26.578 --> 00:08:30.032
But it was this turning point
moment where I got to reassess and

00:08:30.032 --> 00:08:32.366
kind of figure out what
do I want to do next.

00:08:32.366 --> 00:08:37.644
So for me, it was kind of marked by that
milestone and I always loved school so

00:08:37.644 --> 00:08:42.345
the idea of doing something that
could both help me gain new skills,

00:08:42.345 --> 00:08:45.334
new knowledge at a moment of uncertainty.

00:08:45.334 --> 00:08:48.175
So kind of,
seemed like too good to be true.

00:08:48.175 --> 00:08:52.003
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And I remember, I visited Stanford.

00:08:52.003 --> 00:08:55.798
And I had this feeling like I just
stepped on to the GSB campus.

00:08:55.798 --> 00:08:57.166
I don't know if you all felt this.

00:08:57.166 --> 00:09:00.155
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But I loved it immediately.

00:09:00.155 --> 00:09:02.841
Like, everything was beautiful,
but there was this town square and

00:09:02.841 --> 00:09:04.110
people were waving across.

00:09:04.110 --> 00:09:06.258
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I-
&gt;&gt; The Quad, and it was just,

00:09:06.258 --> 00:09:09.248
it seemed really like a cozy community,
and so I think that,

00:09:09.248 --> 00:09:12.783
that also kind of accelerated the,
okay, this is where I want to be now.

00:09:12.783 --> 00:09:15.420
&gt;&gt; Nice.

00:09:15.420 --> 00:09:16.250
Mine's brief.

00:09:16.250 --> 00:09:17.545
Basically, Ditto Marcello.

00:09:17.545 --> 00:09:20.685
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I'll say in particular it just

00:09:20.685 --> 00:09:24.796
felt like the right time after
a couple of years of my previous work

00:09:24.796 --> 00:09:29.239
to just do some soul searching about
future directions and my values.

00:09:29.239 --> 00:09:32.045
So, keep it there.

00:09:32.045 --> 00:09:36.012
The third question that we have is,
I like this one,

00:09:36.012 --> 00:09:39.992
how did you know the GSB was
the right school for you?

00:09:39.992 --> 00:09:41.933
I think we touched on
the that a little bit.

00:09:41.933 --> 00:09:44.726
There's a magic moment, but.

00:09:44.726 --> 00:09:49.932
&gt;&gt; I met people when I was considering
a applying to the MBA program,

00:09:49.932 --> 00:09:54.110
and I met people who have
been to different schools.

00:09:54.110 --> 00:10:00.430
I just found myself being more attracted
to the way that GSBers express themselves.

00:10:00.430 --> 00:10:05.301
And I remember in one of my essays I
wrote that what I like about Stanford

00:10:05.301 --> 00:10:09.118
is that people talked about dreams and
not just goals.

00:10:09.118 --> 00:10:13.829
So it just there was like this aura of
excitement and following your passion, and

00:10:13.829 --> 00:10:17.588
trying to do something good for
the world and not just yourself.

00:10:17.588 --> 00:10:23.049
And that just, yeah,
I was very attracted to that.

00:10:23.049 --> 00:10:27.222
&gt;&gt; I mean, when I visited,
it was just a feeling that I had.

00:10:27.222 --> 00:10:32.761
I think that, that was maybe a piece
of it but I couldn't quite identify it.

00:10:32.761 --> 00:10:34.916
I don't know,
it's hard to answer that specifically.

00:10:34.916 --> 00:10:39.038
&gt;&gt; For me, it sounds funny, but
it was actually going to one of

00:10:39.038 --> 00:10:44.411
the presentations that the GSB admissions
officers does around the cities.

00:10:44.411 --> 00:10:48.482
So I was working in Atlanta at the time,
and I went to the presentation there.

00:10:48.482 --> 00:10:51.126
And what struck me about what she said,
and

00:10:51.126 --> 00:10:54.730
what the alumni who came into
the presentation were like.

00:10:54.730 --> 00:10:57.474
Was that,
this is a place where we think about,

00:10:57.474 --> 00:11:00.025
where people are empowered to think about.

00:11:00.025 --> 00:11:04.125
I think how success is
different from meaning, and

00:11:04.125 --> 00:11:09.285
that by pursuing meaning,
at some point you'll find success.

00:11:09.285 --> 00:11:12.800
At some point, you'll be able to sustain
yourself and your material needs.

00:11:12.800 --> 00:11:16.058
And so worry about your passion, worry
about your spirit, and worry about your,

00:11:16.058 --> 00:11:19.040
like hone in on those values,
and that's what's important.

00:11:19.040 --> 00:11:22.670
And I think getting the option
of my soul searching

00:11:24.730 --> 00:11:27.120
presented to me that way was just so
compelling.

00:11:27.120 --> 00:11:30.680
And I was like, I'm kind of done
with achieving for achieving.

00:11:30.680 --> 00:11:35.630
I'm done with ticking boxes, I really do
want to go to this place where the peers,

00:11:35.630 --> 00:11:38.120
the structure, the institutions,

00:11:38.120 --> 00:11:41.800
the courses are going to push me to do
what is truly important and meaningful.

00:11:41.800 --> 00:11:46.770
So, once I saw that butterfly cocoon
slide on the presentation, I was like,

00:11:46.770 --> 00:11:48.300
yes, that's right.

00:11:48.300 --> 00:11:49.590
All right.

00:11:49.590 --> 00:11:50.170
More questions.

00:11:50.170 --> 00:11:50.740
This is fun.

00:11:51.820 --> 00:11:56.320
How receptive are employers
to LGBT students?

00:11:56.320 --> 00:11:57.560
Do they host events?

00:11:57.560 --> 00:11:59.920
Are there career panels
that are LGBT oriented?

00:12:02.360 --> 00:12:04.120
&gt;&gt; So, as a career switcher.

00:12:04.120 --> 00:12:05.888
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So, in my old job in LGBT rights,

00:12:05.888 --> 00:12:09.806
like diversity and that context was
the straight people that worked for us.

00:12:09.806 --> 00:12:13.293
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But I was really, I feel like as

00:12:13.293 --> 00:12:18.480
I'm pivoting to much more traditional
things at least for the summer.

00:12:18.480 --> 00:12:24.800
I felt like it was an incredible advantage
to have the LGBT targeted networking.

00:12:24.800 --> 00:12:30.677
Like, all of the consulting firms, a lot
of the banks, did targeted dinners or

00:12:30.677 --> 00:12:36.139
receptions on campus or in nearby,
for specifically for LGBT students.

00:12:36.139 --> 00:12:37.472
So I feel like, kind of,

00:12:37.472 --> 00:12:41.940
it's a way to get a little to have like an
internal champion at any of the companies.

00:12:41.940 --> 00:12:46.620
Especially, if you're going to a more
traditional pathway for the summer.

00:12:46.620 --> 00:12:47.440
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:12:47.440 --> 00:12:50.218
&gt;&gt; I felt like it was a total advantage.

00:12:50.218 --> 00:12:50.745
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:12:50.745 --> 00:12:52.652
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:12:52.652 --> 00:12:55.458
&gt;&gt; I guess we both worked in industries
before where we were on the other

00:12:55.458 --> 00:12:56.060
side of this.

00:12:56.060 --> 00:12:57.527
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:12:57.527 --> 00:12:59.055
&gt;&gt; But, yes, from the other side of that.

00:12:59.055 --> 00:13:02.095
I can tell that it really helps
when you attend these events, and

00:13:02.095 --> 00:13:06.495
the access that you get to people who
are working there and partners and so on.

00:13:06.495 --> 00:13:10.085
So it's really,
it can really make a difference for

00:13:10.085 --> 00:13:12.195
going into traditional careers.

00:13:12.195 --> 00:13:13.615
&gt;&gt; Even just reaching out to

00:13:14.810 --> 00:13:19.020
alums that if you know the company that
you're interested in for the summer,

00:13:19.020 --> 00:13:22.210
you have like one additional
filter that you can try on.

00:13:22.210 --> 00:13:26.410
And I found that, I mean GSB alumni
have so responsive, but the LGBT

00:13:26.410 --> 00:13:30.580
alumni have like gone above and beyond
every time that, like 100% of the time.

00:13:30.580 --> 00:13:33.560
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that's a great point and
absolutely true.

00:13:33.560 --> 00:13:39.020
As I was doing this job search,
I completely relied on LGBT alumni for

00:13:39.020 --> 00:13:41.160
contacts and so on, so great ones.

00:13:41.160 --> 00:13:44.857
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think I will just add that
reaching out MBA is another great

00:13:44.857 --> 00:13:48.092
resource for folks that are kind of,
at this life stage and

00:13:48.092 --> 00:13:50.286
looking at different business paths.

00:13:50.286 --> 00:13:53.504
So it's an organization
that serves MBAs and

00:13:53.504 --> 00:13:56.730
perspective MBAs who are LGBTQ identified.

00:13:56.730 --> 00:14:00.108
So, there's a conference every year,
we went, it was tons of fun,

00:14:00.108 --> 00:14:01.813
I think it's in Dallas this year.

00:14:01.813 --> 00:14:07.128
It's also a great chance for
us to connect with both LGBT employers and

00:14:07.128 --> 00:14:13.810
other LGBT MBAs and prospective MBAs from
across the country, well, North America.

00:14:13.810 --> 00:14:16.120
All right,
we'll do the next question then.

00:14:17.610 --> 00:14:21.900
Do any of the classes address LGBT issues?

00:14:24.110 --> 00:14:27.670
I can jump into this one,
because I am actually working on

00:14:27.670 --> 00:14:31.600
an extension that came out
of LGBT content in a class.

00:14:31.600 --> 00:14:36.400
So, I took a class here on, it was
called advanced topics in philanthropy.

00:14:36.400 --> 00:14:40.910
And as a part of that, I wasn't
presented with a particular case or

00:14:40.910 --> 00:14:43.070
something that was LGBT focused.

00:14:43.070 --> 00:14:44.660
But as with most classes here,

00:14:44.660 --> 00:14:48.510
I had a lot of latitude in deciding what
my assignments were going to be on, and

00:14:48.510 --> 00:14:52.890
how I was going to focus my own lens,
and my own experience in the class.

00:14:52.890 --> 00:14:57.140
So I ended up writing a longer
paper about LGBTQ philanthropy.

00:14:57.140 --> 00:14:58.490
Which then the professor went and

00:14:58.490 --> 00:15:02.530
said, this is super interesting,
it's very contemporary, and

00:15:02.530 --> 00:15:05.910
I'd like to actually extend this and
turn it into teaching material.

00:15:05.910 --> 00:15:09.460
So, I think the class at some future
point will actually have that

00:15:09.460 --> 00:15:10.780
as something to teach with.

00:15:10.780 --> 00:15:14.757
But that's one way that I infuse,
by myself, LGBT content in class, and

00:15:14.757 --> 00:15:19.317
professors are, as long as you're working
with something you're excited about,

00:15:19.317 --> 00:15:22.294
professors are super happy
to let you do what you want.

00:15:22.294 --> 00:15:27.257
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I'm not aware of any like
explicitly LGBT classes at the GSB,

00:15:27.257 --> 00:15:31.890
but I know that you can take
classes anywhere at the university.

00:15:31.890 --> 00:15:37.855
And there are LGBT themed classes outside
of the GSB that we're allowed to take.

00:15:37.855 --> 00:15:38.504
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:15:38.504 --> 00:15:40.196
&gt;&gt; Yeah, but I'm not aware of any.

00:15:40.196 --> 00:15:43.679
&gt;&gt; There is, I'm in the class right now,
Jamie would smack me.

00:15:43.679 --> 00:15:45.290
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So

00:15:45.290 --> 00:15:48.910
I am in another course right now
called reputation management.

00:15:48.910 --> 00:15:50.000
And actually as a part of that,

00:15:50.000 --> 00:15:54.200
very recently we've had two
back-to-back LGBT speakers come in.

00:15:54.200 --> 00:15:59.692
So one was, Raymond Bron who is
a Stanford undergrad, graduate.

00:15:59.692 --> 00:16:04.603
Who came in and talked about his online
activism on LGBT rights with YouTube.

00:16:04.603 --> 00:16:09.012
And then a second speaker who came in and
talked to us about how you

00:16:09.012 --> 00:16:12.933
manage your reputation at
the frontier of the movement,

00:16:12.933 --> 00:16:18.504
she was a GSB alum who played a huge role
in the repeal of Don't Ask, Don't Tell.

00:16:18.504 --> 00:16:23.182
And so she actually walked us through
that experience and related it back

00:16:23.182 --> 00:16:27.650
to the class, so I actually have
had some LGBT specific curriculum.

00:16:27.650 --> 00:16:29.360
And then, the next question.

00:16:29.360 --> 00:16:37.091
So how does being an LGBT student at
the GSB relate to your next career move?

00:16:37.091 --> 00:16:42.759
And another way of putting it,
how important is it to be openly LGBT and

00:16:42.759 --> 00:16:48.924
being in an organization that's going to
support that part of your identity?

00:16:48.924 --> 00:16:50.248
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that's really a good one.

00:16:50.248 --> 00:16:53.569
I think for the first part,
as we said, for traditional careers,

00:16:53.569 --> 00:16:57.550
there will be events, and
you can always reach out to alumni.

00:16:57.550 --> 00:17:02.535
But in terms of how it's important
to be in an organization that

00:17:02.535 --> 00:17:07.614
will support it, I think just personally,
I couldn't bear any

00:17:07.614 --> 00:17:12.714
longer to work at an organization
where I had to be in the closet.

00:17:12.714 --> 00:17:18.258
It's just there's so much energy that
you have to pour into hiding things and

00:17:18.258 --> 00:17:23.827
making things up, and it deters the type
of relationships that you've had.

00:17:23.827 --> 00:17:28.831
So again, I didn't go into this
job through an LGBT channel,

00:17:28.831 --> 00:17:32.490
but I definitely made sure that I,
honest and

00:17:32.490 --> 00:17:38.194
open from the beginning about my
sexual orientation and my identity.

00:17:38.194 --> 00:17:43.656
And I think in the end, that's a little
bit of a personal decision for each one.

00:17:43.656 --> 00:17:48.343
A little bit, hopefully, a decision to
try to be a little bit activist and

00:17:48.343 --> 00:17:49.790
help the next person.

00:17:49.790 --> 00:17:54.655
But yeah, I mean, there's so much research
saying that you have to be authentic.

00:17:54.655 --> 00:18:00.316
And that's also something that
the GSB places a lot of emphasis on,

00:18:00.316 --> 00:18:06.079
just authenticity and how that helps
build your leadership style and

00:18:06.079 --> 00:18:10.274
bring others to support you,
so that's my take.

00:18:10.274 --> 00:18:11.598
&gt;&gt; Anything you want to add to that?

00:18:11.598 --> 00:18:14.895
&gt;&gt; I mean, I really agree with.

00:18:14.895 --> 00:18:18.838
For me,
it would be a jawbreaker if I thought that

00:18:18.838 --> 00:18:21.956
the employer didn't support LGBT.

00:18:21.956 --> 00:18:25.155
I almost would that say for me,

00:18:25.155 --> 00:18:30.690
they'd have to have 100 on the HRC index
that measures how well companies do.

00:18:30.690 --> 00:18:34.655
But for me, I'm going to
Goldman Sachs this summer, and

00:18:34.655 --> 00:18:38.127
I recruited through
the traditional channels but

00:18:38.127 --> 00:18:43.329
I feel like part of what was successful
there was I also used the LGBT network

00:18:43.329 --> 00:18:48.481
to get the inside scoop of I see the HRC
score, but what's it really like?

00:18:48.481 --> 00:18:52.906
And then also knowing about how they
had partnered on the LGBT stuff I had

00:18:52.906 --> 00:18:57.628
done before school really gave me the
confidence to believe on the score that

00:18:57.628 --> 00:18:58.321
they had.

00:18:58.321 --> 00:19:02.325
But I can't imagine, for
me, being able to do it,

00:19:02.325 --> 00:19:06.185
because all of my relevant
experience is really gay.

00:19:06.185 --> 00:19:08.020
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; That's your resume.

00:19:08.020 --> 00:19:09.610
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
So I'm automatically

00:19:09.610 --> 00:19:10.760
outed based on what I do.

00:19:10.760 --> 00:19:15.878
I think that there are companies where
that would not be that helpful or

00:19:15.878 --> 00:19:20.577
regions of the world where it might
be much harder for me to work.

00:19:20.577 --> 00:19:23.782
But for me,
I'm willing to make that sacrifice.

00:19:23.782 --> 00:19:28.454
&gt;&gt; And I, if you'll let me add,
I'll even add that what you just said,

00:19:28.454 --> 00:19:33.370
you wouldn't even consider working
in a place that wasn't accepting of

00:19:33.370 --> 00:19:36.454
your identity, that's a great privilege.

00:19:36.454 --> 00:19:41.357
And coming from a country that's a little
bit more conservative in some places

00:19:41.357 --> 00:19:45.961
around that, it strikes me as something
that's really a privilege, and

00:19:45.961 --> 00:19:49.625
in part, something that can
come out of your experience.

00:19:49.625 --> 00:19:54.086
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and in fact, we had an event
with allies yesterday where we,

00:19:54.086 --> 00:19:59.414
it was just basically a workshop where we
had dialogue between LGBTQ students and

00:19:59.414 --> 00:20:04.034
allies at the GSB talking about what
have your experiences been like on

00:20:04.034 --> 00:20:08.531
the ally side and on the LGBT side
in the workplace, in particular?

00:20:08.531 --> 00:20:12.955
And one ally mentioned
her struggle around.

00:20:12.955 --> 00:20:17.576
She worked in a very conservative and
traditional industry where

00:20:17.576 --> 00:20:22.112
someone who was LGBTQ identified
really stood out by his or her or

00:20:22.112 --> 00:20:27.334
your actions, and so she struggled to
say like, what is my place in that?

00:20:27.334 --> 00:20:28.295
And so that, for

00:20:28.295 --> 00:20:33.111
me, really underscored different people
are going to have different priorities and

00:20:33.111 --> 00:20:36.704
trade offs and where they are in
their own coming out journey.

00:20:36.704 --> 00:20:40.325
So I don't want to minimize that either,
it's something that.

00:20:40.325 --> 00:20:43.775
I think we've talked about, but want to
really make sure it comes through in

00:20:43.775 --> 00:20:46.648
the conversation that it is
very individual and contextual.

00:20:46.648 --> 00:20:51.670
&gt;&gt; And it's something that we've talked
a lot about casually over drinks.

00:20:51.670 --> 00:20:54.138
Marco mentions that
oftentimes on Thursdays.

00:20:54.138 --> 00:20:57.090
A lot of the LGBT students
will get together, and

00:20:57.090 --> 00:21:01.698
there's a couple of our classmates that
want to work in the Middle East or that

00:21:01.698 --> 00:21:06.531
are going back to home countries where
it's not quite as safe to be openly LGBT.

00:21:06.531 --> 00:21:10.788
And how do you navigate that,
how do we support one another?

00:21:10.788 --> 00:21:13.986
Talking about,
should I come out to my supervisor or

00:21:13.986 --> 00:21:17.295
my supervisor's supervisor,
however that works?

00:21:17.295 --> 00:21:19.334
So it's a tricky question, but

00:21:19.334 --> 00:21:22.894
it's one that I think that
people are really actively.

00:21:22.894 --> 00:21:25.750
Yeah, we want to support one another.

00:21:25.750 --> 00:21:27.399
&gt;&gt; 100%.

00:21:27.399 --> 00:21:31.194
So thinking about the next question,

00:21:31.194 --> 00:21:35.231
how big is the LGBT community at the GSB?

00:21:35.231 --> 00:21:41.336
How big is it on the campus at large, and
what kind of support, or involvement,

00:21:41.336 --> 00:21:47.189
or interaction does the rest of our
class have with the LGBT community here?

00:21:50.170 --> 00:21:53.659
&gt;&gt; Again, I'll provide the story,
the perspective.

00:21:53.659 --> 00:21:56.620
It's another part of what's been so
amazing.

00:21:56.620 --> 00:22:00.500
I think I've seen it doubled
from my class to your class.

00:22:00.500 --> 00:22:02.630
&gt;&gt; Wow.
&gt;&gt; And then, I don't know how it was for

00:22:02.630 --> 00:22:07.320
your class seems even larger and
definitely louder [LAUGH].

00:22:07.320 --> 00:22:09.291
But yeah, so there's a group, as you said.

00:22:09.291 --> 00:22:10.475
I don't know the exact numbers.

00:22:10.475 --> 00:22:11.195
I don't know.

00:22:11.195 --> 00:22:14.691
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so it's a little tricky,
because obviously,

00:22:14.691 --> 00:22:17.610
it's something that is self-identified.

00:22:17.610 --> 00:22:20.540
People self-identify or
don't in different contexts.

00:22:20.540 --> 00:22:23.400
But what we did report out and
is available through

00:22:23.400 --> 00:22:28.410
reaching out is that I think we're
about 5% of most of the classes.

00:22:28.410 --> 00:22:33.430
Which I think if I remember, the list was
actually pretty high up in terms of, so.

00:22:33.430 --> 00:22:37.980
&gt;&gt; And then, I'm glad there was also
a question about other campus at large,

00:22:37.980 --> 00:22:40.480
because I've enjoyed GradQ so much.

00:22:40.480 --> 00:22:44.898
GradQ is the LGBT student association for
all graduate schools, so

00:22:44.898 --> 00:22:49.700
that includes Stanford law school,
med school, and all the other PhD and

00:22:49.700 --> 00:22:51.655
advanced degrees programs.

00:22:51.655 --> 00:22:53.489
And I've met so
many interesting people there.

00:22:53.489 --> 00:22:59.424
I feel sometimes, my fellow GSB student
get this great opportunity to meet so

00:22:59.424 --> 00:23:04.174
many diverse people from
the social psychology department,

00:23:04.174 --> 00:23:06.475
law students, everything.

00:23:06.475 --> 00:23:10.027
I just really appreciate that
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that's true.

00:23:10.027 --> 00:23:14.310
And then, actually, coincidentally,
tomorrow night, we are having, every once

00:23:14.310 --> 00:23:18.540
in a while, all the LGBT folk in the area
take over a straight bar in Palo Alto.

00:23:18.540 --> 00:23:24.330
So that's actually tomorrow night, and
it's such a pretty sizable community at

00:23:24.330 --> 00:23:30.420
the GSB throughout Standford and
in the immediate South Bay Peninsula area.

00:23:30.420 --> 00:23:32.850
And then of course,
I used to live in San Francisco myself, so

00:23:32.850 --> 00:23:35.960
I go up to the city fairly often to
see friends that are there, and so

00:23:35.960 --> 00:23:38.510
there's obviously that community as well.

00:23:38.510 --> 00:23:42.680
And then to the second part of that
question, what kind of support or

00:23:42.680 --> 00:23:45.340
involvement does the rest
of the class have with us?

00:23:45.340 --> 00:23:49.058
I think we've alluded to this a little
bit with some of the ally events, but

00:23:49.058 --> 00:23:51.077
anything else you'd want to add to that?

00:23:51.077 --> 00:23:56.204
&gt;&gt; I would just say, for me,
it was so striking how different

00:23:56.204 --> 00:24:00.810
it was to step back into
a university environment and

00:24:00.810 --> 00:24:05.324
have had so much change,
like from when I came out.

00:24:05.324 --> 00:24:09.057
So, when I went to university where I
think it was a much more kind of hostile

00:24:09.057 --> 00:24:09.780
experience.

00:24:09.780 --> 00:24:11.490
I mean there was supportive pockets but

00:24:11.490 --> 00:24:16.770
I don't know that it was as welcoming
as I've felt welcomed here,

00:24:16.770 --> 00:24:22.050
and I think about, so every,
once a week there are two people from your

00:24:22.050 --> 00:24:26.500
class that will give what's called a talk
and they give a lot of their life story,

00:24:26.500 --> 00:24:31.000
and you know, obviously every so
often one of those people is LGBT.

00:24:31.000 --> 00:24:36.290
And I feel like the conversations that get
sparked through that institution of talk.

00:24:36.290 --> 00:24:41.430
Which is one of my favorite things about
Stanford, have been really moving for me.

00:24:41.430 --> 00:24:46.360
And then, also just the way that I think
about the support that I have just

00:24:46.360 --> 00:24:49.760
from my section and
how they've welcomed and my boyfriend, so

00:24:49.760 --> 00:24:53.720
we call all of our partners SOs here, and

00:24:53.720 --> 00:24:59.443
I feel like he's been welcomed in so
warmly by all students.

00:24:59.443 --> 00:24:59.979
&gt;&gt; [CROSSTALK]
&gt;&gt; He's lovely.

00:24:59.979 --> 00:25:00.568
&gt;&gt; It's amazing.

00:25:00.568 --> 00:25:03.085
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Thanks.

00:25:03.085 --> 00:25:05.840
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I think so too.

00:25:05.840 --> 00:25:09.290
&gt;&gt; But I think that really
says a lot that like, there

00:25:09.290 --> 00:25:12.770
are straight people in particular reach
out to him before they reach out to me and

00:25:12.770 --> 00:25:17.220
I love that just because I feel like it's
kind of a sign of how things are really

00:25:17.220 --> 00:25:21.640
changing and that feels really different
from when I was an undergraduate.

00:25:21.640 --> 00:25:25.510
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think
&gt;&gt; I'll just add that it's not,

00:25:25.510 --> 00:25:27.200
the GSP is really diversed, right?

00:25:27.200 --> 00:25:29.580
And we mean that in all kinds of ways.

00:25:29.580 --> 00:25:32.960
So I don't want to paint over
the fact that there are people here,

00:25:32.960 --> 00:25:38.070
with different political points of view
with different ideological leanings.

00:25:38.070 --> 00:25:41.150
But I think one thing
that's been super positive

00:25:41.150 --> 00:25:43.790
&gt;&gt; About the GSB is that kind of

00:25:43.790 --> 00:25:49.480
authenticity, and the inquisitive culture
that we have, because I have had lots

00:25:49.480 --> 00:25:54.880
of heart to hearts, and [INAUDIBLE]
conversations

00:25:54.880 --> 00:25:56.560
with people where we have disagreed.

00:25:56.560 --> 00:26:01.330
So, I think the best example would
be a classmate of mine and a friend.

00:26:01.330 --> 00:26:05.030
&gt;&gt; Posted something on Facebook
following the Supreme Court announcement

00:26:05.030 --> 00:26:08.270
of same sex marriage in
the United States where he disagreed.

00:26:09.330 --> 00:26:13.880
And we ended up having a very frank
two hour lunch about what that meant,

00:26:13.880 --> 00:26:19.030
how that landed on me,
how his statement made me feel and

00:26:19.030 --> 00:26:23.620
we walked away actually finding more and
more common ground than I expected and

00:26:23.620 --> 00:26:27.290
we're still close friends and
I still play with his kids.

00:26:27.290 --> 00:26:32.300
So I appreciate those kinds of exchanges
because I recognize that the disagreements

00:26:32.300 --> 00:26:36.760
and shifting polar cool winds will always
be in some way a part of our future, so

00:26:36.760 --> 00:26:41.520
I appreciate having the community
here to have that really open,

00:26:41.520 --> 00:26:43.120
candid dialogue, if anything.

00:26:45.180 --> 00:26:47.270
So moving on to the next question.

00:26:47.270 --> 00:26:53.230
Is GSB pride purely social or do we take
on advocacy work or do other activities?

00:26:54.410 --> 00:26:55.130
&gt;&gt; I want that.

00:26:55.130 --> 00:26:55.750
&gt;&gt; You guys are
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:26:55.750 --> 00:26:57.410
&gt;&gt; We were kind of talking about

00:26:57.410 --> 00:27:01.670
that before the studio session
started because we were thinking about

00:27:01.670 --> 00:27:03.760
&gt;&gt; There's a lot of things a pride

00:27:03.760 --> 00:27:04.430
group could do.

00:27:04.430 --> 00:27:09.280
We could do education around topics
around HB2 and what that means for

00:27:09.280 --> 00:27:13.840
business or you know panels about for
straights allies.

00:27:13.840 --> 00:27:16.990
We could do networking stuff for
helping each other get jobs.

00:27:16.990 --> 00:27:21.530
We could do social stuff which we do a lot
of and the other thing I think we do a lot

00:27:21.530 --> 00:27:27.630
of is If you think of people,
for some of our classmates

00:27:27.630 --> 00:27:32.830
this is the first time where it's really
been safe for people to come out and so

00:27:32.830 --> 00:27:37.010
thinking back to like when I came out how
important having a supportive community

00:27:37.010 --> 00:27:41.880
but also like other support resources,
I think that's one thing that pride does.

00:27:41.880 --> 00:27:46.770
It's not exactly social, but providing
all kinds of support for coming out, and

00:27:46.770 --> 00:27:48.340
like, what does that mean for
the workplace,

00:27:48.340 --> 00:27:50.420
what does that mean in your social life,
with your family.

00:27:52.220 --> 00:27:57.350
And then also advocacy around events like
HB2 which he haven't done as much of,

00:27:57.350 --> 00:28:02.580
but yeah and
then I would kind of call all the events

00:28:02.580 --> 00:28:07.240
you're doing with allies kind of
advocacy slash education and another

00:28:07.240 --> 00:28:10.560
one of the things I'm really excited to
see in recent years, the way you do that.

00:28:10.560 --> 00:28:15.210
It used to be more of a social club, it's
evolved and I've seen it every year and

00:28:15.210 --> 00:28:16.840
with other clubs.

00:28:16.840 --> 00:28:20.760
Always kind of evolving to the needs and
what people are excited about.

00:28:22.160 --> 00:28:25.330
&gt;&gt; Yeah I'd say it's fairly dependent
on the group of people that

00:28:25.330 --> 00:28:26.850
want to take action.

00:28:26.850 --> 00:28:30.260
I think with most clubs here,
they're fairly open.

00:28:30.260 --> 00:28:32.100
You'll find that all
the clubs have ten leaders,

00:28:32.100 --> 00:28:33.355
because everyone wants to be a leader.

00:28:33.355 --> 00:28:35.470
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But I think that works out in a way

00:28:35.470 --> 00:28:39.220
where you share work and people really
take their own personal initiative.

00:28:39.220 --> 00:28:43.770
And so I think for example,
when our year took over the leadership,

00:28:43.770 --> 00:28:46.800
we asked the people that were there,
here, and

00:28:46.800 --> 00:28:52.070
incoming, if five years from now,
when you graduate, what

00:28:52.070 --> 00:28:57.170
will pride have done for
it to be memorable in your experience?

00:28:57.170 --> 00:29:00.290
And then we executed off that, and much
of that was social, much of that was in

00:29:00.290 --> 00:29:03.594
support, some of it was educational,
so, it'll change a bit year to year.

00:29:05.950 --> 00:29:09.600
I think what for me has been really
important has been like an emphasis on

00:29:09.600 --> 00:29:11.920
building a community, whatever that means.

00:29:11.920 --> 00:29:15.590
I feel really connected
to the LGBT people.

00:29:15.590 --> 00:29:17.310
I think particularly in my year.

00:29:17.310 --> 00:29:21.529
But really at the GSB-
&gt;&gt; Also in your third year.

00:29:21.529 --> 00:29:27.645
[LAUGH] [CROSSTALK] All right,
whoa, time really does fly.

00:29:27.645 --> 00:29:32.703
I think we're almost out of time but
before we go when Marcela, any final words

00:29:32.703 --> 00:29:38.130
of advice for folks that are thinking
about applying to the Standford MBA?

00:29:38.130 --> 00:29:40.610
Especially if they're part
of the LGBT community?

00:29:42.930 --> 00:29:44.490
&gt;&gt; I'll just shoot a couple out there.

00:29:46.560 --> 00:29:50.030
Reach out to co workers if you
know someone who is LGBT and

00:29:50.030 --> 00:29:54.780
has a kind of business school they
can be great sources of wisdom.

00:29:54.780 --> 00:29:55.730
More generally,

00:29:55.730 --> 00:30:00.580
just really take the time to enjoy
the process of writing your essay.

00:30:00.580 --> 00:30:05.430
I think part of the decision to come
here was that I had to think so

00:30:05.430 --> 00:30:08.740
hard about it, but at the end I really
felt like I know myself better.

00:30:08.740 --> 00:30:12.920
So it can be hard but really enjoy,
talk to other people about it,

00:30:12.920 --> 00:30:17.080
take the opportunity to get
to know yourself better.

00:30:17.080 --> 00:30:19.750
And yeah, help you enjoy it.

00:30:20.790 --> 00:30:23.540
&gt;&gt; Thanks Marcela
&gt;&gt; I really agree with that

00:30:24.660 --> 00:30:25.790
&gt;&gt; I think one thing I want to flag and

00:30:25.790 --> 00:30:27.160
I know we're not talking
about financial aid, but

00:30:27.160 --> 00:30:32.340
there are specific LGBT scholarships that
are not necessarily specific to Stanford.

00:30:32.340 --> 00:30:35.420
But knowing about the point foundation,
for me was really helpful.

00:30:35.420 --> 00:30:39.160
And then there also is LGBT
specific money at Stanford,

00:30:39.160 --> 00:30:42.500
so if you're able to be
out I think that that's,

00:30:42.500 --> 00:30:46.150
I just wish that had been flagged for
me and the second thing is is

00:30:46.150 --> 00:30:49.500
I love talking to potential applicants
about what you're thinking about.

00:30:49.500 --> 00:30:53.970
So, kind of following on your advice about
is there's someone that you can reach out

00:30:53.970 --> 00:30:59.840
to and ask a little bit to get even
more of a peak into their experience and

00:30:59.840 --> 00:31:02.880
to follow up with more questions
I think that I definitely

00:31:02.880 --> 00:31:05.530
recommend
&gt;&gt; Doing anything you can

00:31:05.530 --> 00:31:08.440
to have those conversations as you're
going through the application process.

00:31:09.490 --> 00:31:14.330
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think my quick tidbit is
just to be authentic to your identity.

00:31:14.330 --> 00:31:18.930
For me, like I said, my professional
interest is in the LGBTQ community, so

00:31:18.930 --> 00:31:21.790
of course that's going to show up at
length in my application, of course that's

00:31:21.790 --> 00:31:24.820
going to show up at length in my essays,
but may or may not be the case for

00:31:24.820 --> 00:31:30.680
you depending on how you want to live and
express that identity and your values.

00:31:30.680 --> 00:31:33.800
So put down what makes sense for you.

00:31:33.800 --> 00:31:36.410
There isn't a magic formula
with your identity nor

00:31:36.410 --> 00:31:40.230
with anything else in the application
I really think it is very holistic.

00:31:41.270 --> 00:31:44.692
So on that note, well,
thank you both of you for joining us.

00:31:44.692 --> 00:31:46.194
&gt;&gt; Thank you.
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:31:46.194 --> 00:31:46.790
And also
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:31:46.790 --> 00:31:48.190
